## LATER — compose_new (deferred, not started)

Reply flow (now proven end-to-end with real send) is NOT to be used as
a template by copy-paste — compose_new is a genuinely different input
shape, converging on the same send step. Design already agreed,
captured here so it doesn't need re-deriving:

- **No mail.raw involved.** Reply derives to/subject/In-Reply-To/
  References from an existing message via `derive_reply_fields()`.
  compose_new has no prior message — to/subject are typed directly
  by the user, no threading headers at all (In-Reply-To/References
  don't apply — not blank, just absent).

- **New routes, not a variant of existing ones:** `GET /compose` (blank
  form: To, Subject, body) and `POST /compose`. Does not touch
  `find_entry`, `generate_raw`, or `derive_reply_fields` — those are
  reply-specific by definition.

- **Converges on the same send step.** Both reply and compose_new end
  up building the same payload shape:
  ```
  {"from": ..., "to": [...], "subject": ..., "text": ...,
   "headers": {"In-Reply-To": ..., "References": ...}}  # reply only
  ```
  compose_new's payload just omits the "headers" block entirely.
  Both call the same `send_json.sh` via the same subprocess pattern
  already proven in `/reply` POST — no new send logic needed.

- **Open question, not yet resolved:** does compose_new need any kind
  of address input helper (recent contacts, etc.) given hand-typing a
  full email address on a T9 keypad is a real usability problem — or
  is plain hand-typing acceptable for now? Flagged during design
  discussion, deliberately not solved yet — reply.sh sidesteps this
  entirely since To is always derived, so this is compose_new's own
  problem to solve, not inherited from reply.

- **Shared helper file** (mail_helpers.sh-style, for reply.sh /
  compose_new.sh if they end up needing common code) — deliberately
  NOT created preemptively. Wait until compose_new exists as real code
  and actual duplication is visible, then extract only what's genuinely
  shared. Reasoning: extracting "common" logic from a single existing
  implementation (reply.sh alone) risks premature abstraction.

## Known gaps carried over from handover v3 (not re-derived here, still open)

- Filter 2 doesn't yet handle base64-encoded bodies or genuine
  multipart/alternative MIME messages (prefer text/plain part).
- Read-flag ("mark as read") sync — deliberately not implemented,
  decision documented in handover v3, not to be revisited without a
  new reason.
- gmap migration into T9A/maps/ — folder scaffolded, still pending,
  lower priority than mail.
